,"followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/atbagan","id":46534421,"login":"atbagan","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ2NTM0NDIx","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/atbagan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/butlerdotdev/butler-portal/commits/256a9c919c546de540682fcedbe999726db2413d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-06-08T19:46:24Z","email":"46534421+atbagan@users.noreply.github.com","name":"atbagan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-06-08T19:46:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(portal): fail hard on auth-init failure + add /_health endpoint (#14)\n\n* feat(portal): fail hard when butler-server auth fails at plugin init\n\nThe previous behavior caught AuthManager.login() failures into a warn-\nlevel log and let plugin init continue. The result: Backstage marked\nthe plugin as initialized, routes were mounted, and butler-server-\ncredential misconfiguration was invisible until a user clicked a Butler\npage (which then returned a vague 502). This is how the 2026-06-07\nincident''s 68-day silent failure was possible.\n\nWith this change, init failure propagates and Backstage marks the\nbutler plugin as failed. The butler routes return 503 (\"Service has\nnot started up yet\") instead of attempting (and failing) the proxy\ncall. Global health endpoints stay 200 (other plugins are fine), so\nthe IDP shell, catalog, and TechDocs continue to work.\n\nThe next commit adds a dedicated /api/butler/_health endpoint\nspecifically for operator monitoring. The chart''s readiness probe is\nintentionally NOT rewired to butler health: a degraded butler plugin\nshould not take down the rest of the portal. Operators wanting butler-\nspecific alerting monitor /api/butler/_health independently.\n\nVerification by inspection (no Backstage init test harness in this\nrepo): the try/catch around authManager.login() is removed; on\nfailure the throw propagates through Backstage''s BackendInitializer\nwhich wraps it in ForwardedError(\"Plugin ''butler'' startup failed\")\npreserving the original AuthManager error as cause.\n\n* feat(portal): add /api/butler/_health endpoint for monitoring\n\nExposes AuthManager''s current state on an unauthenticated endpoint\nsuitable for monitoring tools. Returns 200 when butler-portal has a\nnon-expired butler-server JWT, 503 when degraded. Includes lastError\nwhen degraded to aid diagnostics.\n\nThe endpoint complements (but does not replace) the previous commit''s\nfail-hard init behavior. Init failure makes butler routes return 503\nimmediately at startup, but the structured /_health route only exists\nAFTER plugin init succeeds. For deploy-time failures (misconfigured\ncredentials, butler-server unreachable at boot), butler routes return\nBackstage''s generic lifecycle 503 with body \"Service has not started\nup yet\"; the structured /_health body applies to runtime-class\ndegradation only.\n\nRuntime drift handling: scheduleRefresh in AuthManager attempts ONE\nretry 30 seconds after a refresh failure, then stops. Further refresh\nfailures are unhandled. /_health flips to 503 only after the held\ntoken''s exp claim passes. Persistent runtime degradation is a known\ngap, tracked as followup (the right shape is either indefinite retry\nwith exponential backoff while reporting degraded via /_health, OR\naccept single-shot and document it as the intended contract).\n\nThe endpoint is intentionally NOT wired to the chart''s readiness probe.\nThe probe stays on the global /healthcheck path so a degraded butler\nplugin does not take down the IDP shell, catalog, or TechDocs.\nOperators wanting butler-specific alerting should monitor\n/api/butler/_health from Prometheus blackbox exporter or equivalent,\nAND watch for the generic \"Service has not started up yet\" 503 on\nany butler route since that signals a deploy-time failure where the\nstructured /_health route does not exist.\n\nImplementation:\n- AuthManager: new lastError field captured in login()''s outer\n  try/catch wrapper around the existing loginInternal logic. Success\n  clears lastError; failure records it before re-throwing.\n- AuthManager: new getHealthSnapshot() method returning a typed\n  snapshot {authenticated, tokenExpiresAt?, lastError?}. Does not throw\n  under any input state.\n- router.ts: GET /_health calls getHealthSnapshot, returns 200 or 503.\n  Inline JSDoc documents the forward-compat sensitivity of lastError\n  (if butler-server''s error responses ever include sensitive details,\n  they would leak via this unauthenticated endpoint).\n- plugin.ts: addAuthPolicy({path: ''/_health'', allow: ''unauthenticated''})\n  before mounting the router. Inline comment documents that Backstage''s\n  policy path matching is prefix-based, so future /_health/* routes\n  would be unauthenticated by default.\n\nSensitivity review: lastError contains the message from butler-server''s\nHTTP error responses (e.g., \"butler-server login failed: 401\nUnauthorized - {\\\"error\\\":\\\"Invalid credentials\\\"}\"). No credentials,\ntokens, or other sensitive material are included TODAY. Forward-compat\nrisk noted in code comments.\n\nTests: 7 new tests in __tests__/AuthManager.test.ts cover the snapshot\nstate machine (no-token, valid-token, expired-token, expiry-buffer,\nlastError-recorded, last-action-wins, no-exp-claim). Login-roundtrip\ntests are deferred to a future testing-infrastructure session that adds\nfetch mocking; this commit''s scope is the snapshot logic in isolation.\n\nAll 26 tests pass (19 validation + 7 AuthManager). yarn tsc clean.\nhelm template still renders.\n\n* chore(portal): bump chart to 0.2.3 and document _health monitoring\n\nChart 0.2.3 ships the v0.2.3 portal image, which adds the fail-hard\ninit behavior and the /api/butler/_health endpoint introduced in the\ntwo preceding commits.\n\nREADME updates:\n- New \"Monitoring\" section explaining the endpoint response shapes\n- Important monitoring note: the structured /api/butler/_health body\n  is only available AFTER plugin init succeeds. For deploy-time\n  failures, butler routes return Backstage''s generic lifecycle 503\n  (\"Service has not started up yet\") and the structured /_health\n  route does not exist. Operators monitor both signals.\n- Example Prometheus blackbox exporter config snippet\n- Failure-modes table with F1-F5 labels. F3 framing corrected to\n  reflect actual code behavior: scheduleRefresh retries ONCE after\n  30s (not indefinitely), then stops. Persistent runtime degradation\n  is tracked as followup.\n- Updated opt-out section reference text to align with the v0.2.2\n  narrowing already shipped (no behavior change, just clarifying prose)\n\nNote that the chart''s readiness probe still targets /healthcheck (the\nglobal Backstage path), NOT /api/butler/_health. This is intentional:\na degraded butler plugin should not take down the IDP shell, catalog,\nor TechDocs. Operators wanting butler-specific alerting wire their own\nmonitoring (Prometheus blackbox, Pingdom, etc.) to /api/butler/_health.\n\nNo breaking change. Chart values are unchanged. Deployments upgrading\nfrom 0.2.2 see no shape change, only the runtime behavior change from\nthe underlying image bump.","tree":{"sha":"148fa288ac53255e6b0d270bd4f7d56cacf68a43","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/butlerdotdev/butler-portal/git/trees/148fa288ac53255e6b0d270bd4f7d56cacf68a43"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/butlerdotdev/butler-portal/git/commits/256a9c919c546de540682fcedbe999726db2413d","verification":{"payload":"tree 148fa288ac53255e6b0d270bd4f7d56cacf68a43\nparent 595df4c008c96b5533d97fde6e6befafb07daf2e\nauthor atbagan <46534421+atbagan@users.noreply.github.com> 1780947984 -0500\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1780947984 -0500\n\nfeat(portal): fail hard on auth-init failure + add /_health endpoint (#14)\n\n* feat(portal): fail hard when butler-server auth fails at plugin init\n\nThe previous behavior caught AuthManager.login() failures into a warn-\nlevel log and let plugin init continue. The result: Backstage marked\nthe plugin as initialized, routes were mounted, and butler-server-\ncredential misconfiguration was invisible until a user clicked a Butler\npage (which then returned a vague 502). This is how the 2026-06-07\nincident''s 68-day silent failure was possible.\n\nWith this change, init failure propagates and Backstage marks the\nbutler plugin as failed. The butler routes return 503 (\"Service has\nnot started up yet\") instead of attempting (and failing) the proxy\ncall. Global health endpoints stay 200 (other plugins are fine), so\nthe IDP shell, catalog, and TechDocs continue to work.\n\nThe next commit adds a dedicated /api/butler/_health endpoint\nspecifically for operator monitoring. The chart''s readiness probe is\nintentionally NOT rewired to butler health: a degraded butler plugin\nshould not take down the rest of the portal. Operators wanting butler-\nspecific alerting monitor /api/butler/_health independently.\n\nVerification by inspection (no Backstage init test harness in this\nrepo): the try/catch around authManager.login() is removed; on\nfailure the throw propagates through Backstage''s BackendInitializer\nwhich wraps it in ForwardedError(\"Plugin ''butler'' startup failed\")\npreserving the original AuthManager error as cause.\n\n* feat(portal): add /api/butler/_health endpoint for monitoring\n\nExposes AuthManager''s current state on an unauthenticated endpoint\nsuitable for monitoring tools. Returns 200 when butler-portal has a\nnon-expired butler-server JWT, 503 when degraded. Includes lastError\nwhen degraded to aid diagnostics.\n\nThe endpoint complements (but does not replace) the previous commit''s\nfail-hard init behavior. Init failure makes butler routes return 503\nimmediately at startup, but the structured /_health route only exists\nAFTER plugin init succeeds. For deploy-time failures (misconfigured\ncredentials, butler-server unreachable at boot), butler routes return\nBackstage''s generic lifecycle 503 with body \"Service has not started\nup yet\"; the structured /_health body applies to runtime-class\ndegradation only.\n\nRuntime drift handling: scheduleRefresh in AuthManager attempts ONE\nretry 30 seconds after a refresh failure, then stops. Further refresh\nfailures are unhandled. /_health flips to 503 only after the held\ntoken''s exp claim passes. Persistent runtime degradation is a known\ngap, tracked as followup (the right shape is either indefinite retry\nwith exponential backoff while reporting degraded via /_health, OR\naccept single-shot and document it as the intended contract).\n\nThe endpoint is intentionally NOT wired to the chart''s readiness probe.\nThe probe stays on the global /healthcheck path so a degraded butler\nplugin does not take down the IDP shell, catalog, or TechDocs.\nOperators wanting butler-specific alerting should monitor\n/api/butler/_health from Prometheus blackbox exporter or equivalent,\nAND watch for the generic \"Service has not started up yet\" 503 on\nany butler route since that signals a deploy-time failure where the\nstructured /_health route does not exist.\n\nImplementation:\n- AuthManager: new lastError field captured in login()''s outer\n  try/catch wrapper around the existing loginInternal logic. Success\n  clears lastError; failure records it before re-throwing.\n- AuthManager: new getHealthSnapshot() method returning a typed\n  snapshot {authenticated, tokenExpiresAt?, lastError?}. Does not throw\n  under any input state.\n- router.ts: GET /_health calls getHealthSnapshot, returns 200 or 503.\n  Inline JSDoc documents the forward-compat sensitivity of lastError\n  (if butler-server''s error responses ever include sensitive details,\n  they would leak via this unauthenticated endpoint).\n- plugin.ts: addAuthPolicy({path: ''/_health'', allow: ''unauthenticated''})\n  before mounting the router. Inline comment documents that Backstage''s\n  policy path matching is prefix-based, so future /_health/* routes\n  would be unauthenticated by default.\n\nSensitivity review: lastError contains the message from butler-server''s\nHTTP error responses (e.g., \"butler-server login failed: 401\nUnauthorized - {\\\"error\\\":\\\"Invalid credentials\\\"}\"). No credentials,\ntokens, or other sensitive material are included TODAY. Forward-compat\nrisk noted in code comments.\n\nTests: 7 new tests in __tests__/AuthManager.test.ts cover the snapshot\nstate machine (no-token, valid-token, expired-token, expiry-buffer,\nlastError-recorded, last-action-wins, no-exp-claim). Login-roundtrip\ntests are deferred to a future testing-infrastructure session that adds\nfetch mocking; this commit''s scope is the snapshot logic in isolation.\n\nAll 26 tests pass (19 validation + 7 AuthManager). yarn tsc clean.\nhelm template still renders.\n\n* chore(portal): bump chart to 0.2.3 and document _health monitoring\n\nChart 0.2.3 ships the v0.2.3 portal image, which adds the fail-hard\ninit behavior and the /api/butler/_health endpoint introduced in the\ntwo preceding commits.\n\nREADME updates:\n- New \"Monitoring\" section explaining the endpoint response shapes\n- Important monitoring note: the structured /api/butler/_health body\n  is only available AFTER plugin init succeeds. For deploy-time\n  failures, butler routes return Backstage''s generic lifecycle 503\n  (\"Service has not started up yet\") and the structured /_health\n  route does not exist. Operators monitor both signals.\n- Example Prometheus blackbox exporter config snippet\n- Failure-modes table with F1-F5 labels. F3 framing corrected to\n  reflect actual code behavior: scheduleRefresh retries ONCE after\n  30s (not indefinitely), then stops. Persistent runtime degradation\n  is tracked as followup.\n- Updated opt-out section reference text to align with the v0.2.2\n  narrowing already shipped (no behavior change, just clarifying prose)\n\nNote that the chart''s readiness probe still targets /healthcheck (the\nglobal Backstage path), NOT /api/butler/_health. This is intentional:\na degraded butler plugin should not take down the IDP shell, catalog,\nor TechDocs. Operators wanting butler-specific alerting wire their own\nmonitoring (Prometheus blackbox, Pingdom, etc.) to /api/butler/_health.\n\nNo breaking change. Chart values are unchanged. Deployments upgrading\nfrom 0.2.2 see no shape change, only the runtime behavior change from\nthe underlying image bump.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqJxwQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANsAQAFjPnongPNqKDf+so7XoFwOf\nrSODx7rEyTzjX7e11R5RaaKQA8eYSMRFpu8g/cr/k9zr7n2mkaN4x1nB093kl54I\njckIb/TaaX+9AeAHSNE5RpFnaMH8OOHjlVwhaMkRvCrEwxaOAhuinfRN9b0R4RPz\nJ220s6402+PHoW1OdoDe72krMhFkLh/CKuRS/Lia011tFvhduSuspFUCWiO1BTMF\n3r3SCCX+/7PFhdtJk7yWuoy61JKYIzyK7bIRRv2++RzXfCTdXXUF5oQk+b0X8ddt\nWoKXAMrQPX6lVGn4iV9K04z2ZletRtwPh1jSZRJNsEt2EOnJ087AALtyCNAEGy7Q\nInFilf8nEXXYoDJBGkQzs53qJvI11FmeCFcnkCpBAIrEMHeJG1GWUip7DZHl0lp0\neY57xmTubtA8PYV5YaoSstYd0xK6IAdYppGJ3n9rxMBcEdRW93CaAqMQoGyRj8Gs\nVzSAGq4XWPL558D+8tE60Z2k4fT4vCctV9PYv6N0OQd9CEZq0rUuPTGiZn/mUpUz\nFrgdmTxcUmWvWhLNlBOqda4kNLcrSoNEThaM5v/wr2NcZMuAb0eofXz1N08dCLBY\nqVO7R/RjXaTxQiMWIlSDK+9Bgb12/pn23o3rYVfibLXM30+9jbiqO4/aMsvmwfOs\npNyVSJTF04tk+3E0Nag9\n=IUyE\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-06-08T19:46:25Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://a